catchya — a Linux kernel for a Zen 5 desktop laptop
===================================================

This is a working fork of the Linux kernel, not a distro patch dump. `master`
tracks torvalds/linux and carries a curated optimisation stack on top of it:
the CachyOS 7.4 desktop set, the BORE scheduler, and a small number of
memory-management and scheduler changes that exist here because they were
measured here.

The goal is narrow and specific: make a 7.4 kernel that is faster in the two
places a developer laptop actually feels the kernel (wakeup latency and fault
latency), keeps idle power low on an AMD Strix Point SoC, and stays rebasable
against upstream release cycles instead of drifting into a private tree.

What this tree is
-----------------

    master = torvalds/linux (merged at c463b80881fb, 7.4-rc2 era)
           + the CachyOS 7.4-rc1-1 desktop stack, cherry-pick -x
           + local fixes and measurement tooling
           + the catchya mm, scheduler and AMD changes

    version 7.4.0-rc1      package linux-zeus 7.4.rc1-1
    modules 7.4.0-rc1-1-zeus

Every carried commit keeps its "(cherry picked from commit ...)" trailer, so
`git log --grep "cherry picked from"` is the provenance index. Adaptations
are squashed into the commit they belong to, never added as a follow-up fixup.

What is optimised, and what each item buys
------------------------------------------

Scheduling — interactive responsiveness under load

* BORE scheduler (`CONFIG_SCHED_BORE`). Burst-oriented burstable fair
  scheduling: boosts the sleeper and the bursty task over the CPU hog, which
  is what makes a desktop stay responsive while a build saturates the box.
  CachyOS dropped BORE for 7.4; this tree keeps it, rebased, because the
  alternative on 7.4 is stock EEVDF.
* POC idle selector (`CONFIG_SCHED_POC_SELECTOR`). Cached-bitmap replacement
  for the idle-CPU scan in `select_idle_sibling()`: same placement decision,
  fewer cycles spent deciding, so a wakeup reaches a CPU sooner.
* Cache-affine futex wake (`CONFIG_SCHED_FUTEX_AFFINE`). A futex wake is a
  producer/consumer handoff, but the scheduler never saw that and could resume
  the wakee in a different LLC from the waker. The hint prefers an idle CPU in
  the waker's LLC and falls back when there is none, so it can only move a
  wakee onto a CPU that was already idle. Kconfig default is off; the package
  enables it. The placement change is proven, the latency win still needs
  silicon, and `/proc/sys/kernel/sched_futex_affine` turns it off for an A/B.

Memory — latency in the fault path

* THP asynchronous defrag (`CONFIG_THP_ASYNC_DEFRAG`). The compiled default
  for `transparent_hugepage/defrag` becomes `defer`: a 2 MiB fault that cannot
  be served immediately falls back to base pages and hands defragmentation to
  `kcompactd`, instead of stalling the faulting task in direct compaction.
* Idle page pre-zeroing (`CONFIG_PREZERO_PAGES`). While a CPU is idle it zeroes
  a small batch of its own per-CPU free pages, so an anonymous fault later
  takes an already-zero page and skips the clear. Same bytes written, moved
  off the fault path; the zero invariant is list membership, and every drain
  path degrades to stock behaviour.
* MGLRU stays on and tuned through `CONFIG_CACHY` (caps `0x0007`, swappiness
  100, a 100-jiffy minimum TTL). Audited rather than patched: there is no
  upstream MGLRU work to backport past this base.

Graphics and SoC — Strix Point / RDNA 3.5

* GFXOFF entry delay (`amdgpu.gfx_off_delay_ms`, 0-1000 ms, default 100). A
  tunable, not a policy change: the request still goes through
  `amdgpu_dpm_set_powergating_by_smu()` and its return value still gates
  `gfx_off_state`. Lower it for a bursty workload, raise it for idle power.
* DCN 3.5 and TTM UMA behaviour is verified stock: `apu_prefer_gtt`, the 2 MiB
  beneficial allocation order and the no-reclaim path for other orders are
  already upstream, so nothing was patched there. Commit-path pruning was
  refused: no reference patch, and the failure mode is a dead display.

Desktop and platform

* BBR3 TCP congestion control (`CONFIG_TCP_CONG_BBR3`), zram with the zstd
  backend, VESA DSC and passive VRR for the panel, AMD P-state in `guided`
  mode by default, and the PCIe/USB/media fixes the CachyOS desktop set
  carries for this class of machine.
* One local correctness fix is carried because it is a build blocker, not a
  performance change: `max96717` bitfield overflow in the pinconf setter,
  which ThinLTO constant-folding turns into a link failure. Submitted
  upstream separately.

Build and package
-----------------

The kernel is built by the companion recipe (linux-cachyos-recipe), which
consumes this repository as its source:

    clang + ThinLTO          -march=native -mtune=native
    AutoFDO                  -falign-functions=32 -falign-loops=32
    Propeller                -ffp-contract=fast -funroll-loops
                             -mprefer-vector-width=256

    HZ=600                   PREEMPT_LAZY
    CONFIG_CACHY=y           TRANSPARENT_HUGEPAGE=always
    INIT_ON_ALLOC on         NTSYNC=m

`pgo-in-one-go.sh` in the recipe runs the five-stage AutoFDO + Propeller
pipeline (baseline build, guest profile under `perf kvm`, instrumented build,
Propeller profiles, final `makepkg`). Out-of-tree kernel builds use `O=` as
usual; see Documentation/kbuild/ for the build system itself.

Measurement before claims
-------------------------

The rule that shapes this tree: a change earns a commit only if it moves
something deterministic, otherwise it stays on a topic branch or is refused
with the number that killed it. `tools/exp/` is the rig — a paired A/B
runner, a recorded noise floor for this laptop, a fault-latency probe, a
boot harness that runs a probe as PID 1 under QEMU, and probes for `ntsync`
and the GFXOFF parameter. Where a claim needs hardware the tree does not have
in a VM (GFXOFF residency, real CCX wake latency, DCN timings), the record
says so instead of quoting a QEMU number.

Ground rules
------------

* Nothing is pushed without the owner's say-so, and automation never adds a
  `Signed-off-by`; only a human certifies the DCO.
* One topic branch per change, each bisectable and buildable on its own;
  `master` must build and boot before an experiment branch is cut.
* AI-assisted contributions follow Documentation/process/coding-assistants.rst
  and carry a `Co-authored-by` trailer, never a sign-off.

Where the detail lives
----------------------

    docs/out-of-tree-patches.md   ledger of every patch this tree carries:
                                  provenance, touch surface, measurement,
                                  retirement condition, rebase rules
    docs/cachyos-stack.md         the CachyOS stack, commit by commit, and how
                                  this tree differs from the release
    docs/JOURNAL.md               dated one-line pointers to action reports
    .logs/action-*.md             per-task evidence: commands, results, what
                                  could not be verified
    tools/exp/                    measurement and boot-test harness

If you are an AI coding assistant, those four are the contract: read the
ledger before adding an optimisation, and record the measurement that
justified it.

